        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "O9Bl8k",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 402,
          "name": "cap",
          "fill": "$text-low",
          "content": "3-WAY SPLIT — CARD SHRINKS",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "600"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "nqFEi",
          "x": 376,
          "y": 402,
          "name": "cap",
          "fill": "$text-low",
          "content": "AGENT PAUSED · AGENT VIEW",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "600"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "uloD3",
          "x": 848,
          "y": 402,
          "name": "cap",
          "fill": "$text-low",
          "content": "SAME CHAT · TERMINAL VIEW — STILL LIVE",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "600"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "O5Qwrg",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 752,
          "name": "cap_notes",
          "fill": "$text-low",
          "content": "RULES",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "600"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "aR7aw",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 774,
          "name": "notes",
          "fill": "$text-mid",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 640,
          "content": "PER VIEW, NOT PER PANE — with the strip, a pane no longer has one lifecycle. Each view ends on its own and the overlay belongs to that view's body. The strip above it stays fully live: the agent can sit paused while a terminal streams, and switching views is how you get from one to the other. Nothing about a paused agent dims or disables its terminals.\n\nAGENT VIEW PAUSED — “Chat paused” card in the agent view only. The strip's agent item flips its dot to $text-low and its label to “stopped”, and the trailing control flips Stop → Resume (play glyph, $accent). Terminal items are untouched.\n\nTERMINAL VIEW EXITED — “Shell exited” card (Restart / Close) in that terminal's view only, with exit code · shell · cwd. The agent keeps running and keeps its idle/working dot; the terminal item never carries a status dot either way.\n\nRESUME SEMANTICS — Resume restarts only the agent process. Child terminals are independent PTYs: they survive the pause and are not respawned, restarted, or re-ordered by it. Archive is the one action that takes the whole chat, terminals included.",
          "lineHeight": 1.55,
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 12,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "xacgM",
          "x": 696,
          "y": 774,
          "name": "notes",
          "fill": "$text-mid",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 640,
          "content": "SIZING — the card is w_full inside a 16 px gutter, capped at 420. In a half-split it reads as a compact centered card; in a 3-way split it shrinks with the pane instead of clipping, which is exactly what today's fixed sheet cannot do.\n\nNARROW STRIPS — below roughly 360 px the strip drops its trailing control first, then collapses terminal labels to icons. Losing the Resume control costs nothing: the card in the body carries Resume and Archive, and ⌘W / ⌘T keep working when a control is hidden.\n\nREPLACES — today's ended overlay is a bottom-anchored sheet (max-w 672, pb 56) over a 70 % $bg scrim, left aligned with pause / play / archive glyphs (ui/session_overlay.rs:158). The proposal keeps its copy verbatim — “Chat paused” + the ended_subtitle strings from surfaces/chat_lifecycle.rs:52 — and changes only the composition: centered in the view, compact, symmetric, no scrim.\n\nOPEN — whether to keep the pause/play/archive glyphs (dropped here for a calmer card) and whether the scrim should return so the transcript behind stays readable. Both are cheap to add back; neither changes the per-view rule above.",
          "lineHeight": 1.55,
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 12,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        }
      ]
    },
