        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "B7crm",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 2046,
          "name": "cap",
          "fill": "$text-low",
          "content": "QUIT AND RELAUNCH",
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontWeight": "600"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "CeAOp",
          "x": 24,
          "y": 2068,
          "name": "notes",
          "fill": "$text-mid",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 700,
          "content": "THE TREE SURVIVES — nodes, each node's layout, pane order, titles and sizes all come back from the nodes table. Relaunch restores the arrangement; only the processes have to be decided.\n\nTERMINAL PANES COME BACK LIVE — a fresh shell at the row's recorded cwd, no command replay and no scrollback. This is Zed's terminal behaviour and the right default here: a shell has no conversation to lose, so respawning is free and lossless — you get a prompt exactly where you left it, and a split full of shells is usable the second the window opens.\n\nCHAT PANES COME BACK PAUSED — the “Chat paused” card with Resume, because resuming a chat costs an agent process and replays a conversation, which is a choice rather than a default. If Settings → resume on launch is on, they auto-resume instead and this card never appears.\n\nLAUNCH COST — this is why the asymmetry matters. Auto-resume runs sequentially with a 300 ms stagger (AUTO_RESUME_STAGGER_MS) and claude-code spawns additionally serialise behind a 1500 ms launch gate (CLAUDE_LAUNCH_GATE_GRACE), so N agents cost roughly N × 1.8 s before the last one is live. Shells skip both — they are plain forks — so a split of terminals costs milliseconds. Keeping chat resume opt-in is what keeps a busy workspace from taking a minute to come back.",
          "lineHeight": 1.55,
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 12,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "vgmCM",
          "x": 756,
          "y": 2068,
          "name": "notes",
          "fill": "$text-mid",
          "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
          "width": 700,
          "content": "WHAT THIS CHANGES IN CODE — the asymmetry is deliberate and does not exist today. `consume_launch_claims` clears every pending claim when resume-on-launch is off (bootstrap.rs:196), so shells have to be exempted from that gate while chats stay behind it. The spawn itself needs nothing new: `resume_plan` already falls through to `ResumePlan::fresh()` for a non-catalog runtime, so resuming a \"shell\" row is a fresh spawn, and `sessions.cwd` already carries the directory.\n\nIF THE CWD IS GONE, SPLIT BY RUNTIME — the hard error `spawn_direct_inner` raises today is right for an AGENT and wrong for a SHELL. Its stated reason is agent-specific: portable-pty silently substitutes $HOME, which strands an autonomous process in the wrong repo and breaks codex rollout capture, because the rollout cwd can never match the row. Neither applies to a human at a prompt.\n\nSO: a chat view keeps the hard failure and comes back on its ended card with Restart. A terminal view falls back instead of dying — a deleted worktree should not leave a permanently un-restartable cell — but the fallback is ours, resolved before the fork, never portable-pty's implicit one. Walk to the nearest existing ancestor of the recorded cwd, else the project directory, else $HOME.\n\nAND IT IS NEVER SILENT — Runner writes the substitution into the terminal itself as its first two lines, before the shell's own output, the way a terminal would. Runner already owns the parser (`feed_output`), so this is a synthetic write, not a banner competing with the identity line.\n\nTITLES — a view the user renamed keeps its name. One auto-named after its command shows the shell name again, because the command is not replayed.",
          "lineHeight": 1.55,
          "fontFamily": "$font-ui",
          "fontSize": 12,
          "fontWeight": "normal"
        },
